# A MIGRATION'S BYTES ARE PUBLISHED, so nothing may rewrite them on the way to a
# working tree.
#
# internal/state/migrations/*.sql carry the exact statement bytes every existing
# deployment recorded a sha256 of. A line-ending conversion on checkout — which is
# what core.autocrlf does on a Windows or a misconfigured machine — would change
# every checksum in the file, and the control plane would refuse to start with
# "migrations are append-only and must never be edited" against a ledger that is
# perfectly fine.
#
# `text eol=lf` rather than `-text`: it pins the working tree to LF in both
# directions AND keeps the files textual, so a new migration still reviews as a
# readable diff. internal/state/migrationfiles.go refuses a carriage return
# outright, which is what makes the failure loud if anything gets past this.
internal/state/migrations/*.sql text eol=lf

# THE SAME REASON, ONE FILE OVER. scripts/kernel/check-config.sh is moby's script
# vendored verbatim and pinned by sha256; the release gate refuses to run a copy
# that does not hash to the pin. A line-ending conversion on checkout changes every
# byte of it, so the gate would report an unreviewed edit against a file nobody
# touched -- and the remedy it names, restoring the file with git, would not help.
scripts/kernel/check-config.sh text eol=lf

# GITHUB'S RUNNER-IMAGES TREE, VENDORED BYTE FOR BYTE. Each file must hash to the
# blob id the pinned commit gives it (scripts/runner-images/BLOBS), so no
# line-ending conversion may touch any of it: `-text` leaves every byte as the
# commit had it.
scripts/runner-images/upstream/** -text
